// Firestore security rules for Hook, Line and Sentence.
//
// THE GAME NO LONGER USES FIRESTORE AT ALL. Cloud saves were removed on
// 2026-09-05: there is no Firebase config in `config.js`, no SDK import, no
// sign-in and no write path. Every save lives in the browser's localStorage.
// This file exists because deleting it would not have changed anything that
// matters: the rules that are LIVE are the ones published in the Firebase
// console, and until they are replaced, any Google account on earth can still
// create documents in the `typingFishing` collection of a project shared with
// Family Hub. The game's URL has been handed to friends and family.
//
// ⚠️  MATT: THIS IS THE ONE STEP CODE CANNOT DO. Publish the block below.
//
//   1. Firebase console -> Firestore Database -> Rules.
//   2. Find your existing `match /databases/{database}/documents { … }` block.
//   3. REPLACE your current `match /typingFishing/{profileId} { … }` block
//      with the one below. Leave every Family Hub rule alone: pasting this
//      whole file over your ruleset would delete them and break Family Hub.
//   4. Publish.
//
// After that, nothing can read or write `typingFishing` through the client
// API, including the game, including you. Documents already there are not
// deleted by this: they are simply unreachable from a browser, and the console
// (which bypasses rules) can still read or export them if you want the old
// saves before you clear the collection.

match /typingFishing/{profileId} {
  // Deny by default and say so out loud, rather than leaving the collection
  // to a broad `request.auth != null` that authorises strangers. This is the
  // whole rule on purpose: an allowlist would still be a door, and there is
  // nothing left on the game's side that needs to walk through one.
  allow read, write: if false;
}

// ── For reference only: the shape your full ruleset should have after step 3.
// ── (Your real Family Hub rules go where the comment is; don't lose them.)
//
// rules_version = '2';
// service cloud.firestore {
//   match /databases/{database}/documents {
//
//     // … your existing Family Hub match blocks stay here, untouched …
//
//     match /typingFishing/{profileId} { allow read, write: if false; }
//   }
// }
//
// If cloud saves ever come back, they need their OWN Firebase project rather
// than this one reopened: the blast radius here is Family Hub's data, and that
// is the thing no game feature is worth risking. FIRESTORE.md keeps the schema
// and the setup that would be needed.
